::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Topic: peer feedback. Which sentence is the clearest perspective for an oral presentation? \{
~A. Peer feedback is the topic of this presentation.#Incorrect. This names the topic but gives no point of view.
~B. Many students write papers.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not state a presentation perspective.
~C. Feedback happens in school sometimes.#Incorrect. This is vague and not focused enough.
=D. Peer feedback helps writers revise because classmates can notice unclear ideas the writer may miss.#Correct. This is a focused perspective that can guide the whole presentation.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.07
Perspective: Reading aloud helps students catch confusing sentences. Which detail best supports the perspective? \{
=A. Hearing a sentence can reveal missing words or awkward phrasing.#Correct. This directly supports the perspective about reading aloud.
~B. Some classrooms have bookshelves.#Incorrect. This is school-related but does not support the perspective.
~C. Students use pencils in many classes.#Incorrect. This detail is unrelated to reading aloud.
~D. Sentences can be written in notebooks.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not show why reading aloud helps.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.08 U06 L03 Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.08
Perspective: Students should use study groups before tests. Opposing view: Some students focus better alone. Which response is strongest? \{
~A. Students who study alone are wrong.#Incorrect. This is unfair and does not respond thoughtfully.
~B. Study groups are always better in every situation.#Incorrect. This is too absolute and ignores the opposing view.
=C. Some students focus well alone, but study groups can still help students hear explanations and test their understanding.#Correct. This fairly acknowledges the opposing view and returns to the main perspective.
~D. Tests are part of many classes.#Incorrect. This does not address the opposing view.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L03 Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Which phrase is most appropriate for an academic oral presentation? \{
~A. a bunch of random stuff#Incorrect. This phrase is informal and unfocused.
=B. coherent support for a clear perspective#Correct. This phrase uses precise academic vocabulary connected to the lesson skill.
~C. things that are kind of helpful#Incorrect. This is vague and weakens precision.
~D. school stuff we do#Incorrect. This does not use academic vocabulary.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.07 U06 L03 Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
A speaker repeats the phrase "small steps create real progress" throughout a presentation. What is the rhetorical purpose of this choice? \{
~A. To remove all organization from the presentation#Incorrect. Repetition can support organization when used purposefully.
~B. To avoid having a perspective#Incorrect. The repeated phrase actually reinforces a perspective.
~C. To change the topic every few seconds#Incorrect. Repetition usually keeps focus rather than changing topics.
=D. To reinforce the central perspective for the audience#Correct. Repetition can help listeners remember the speaker's main point.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.07
Which detail should be removed from a presentation about how tutoring helps students review difficult concepts? \{
~A. Tutoring lets students ask questions about confusing steps.#Incorrect. This supports the perspective.
~B. Tutors can explain a concept in different words.#Incorrect. This supports the perspective.
=C. The tutoring room has a poster with blue letters.#Correct. This detail is unrelated to how tutoring helps students review concepts.
~D. Students can practice problems with feedback during tutoring.#Incorrect. This supports the perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.08
Which plan has the strongest coherent focus? \{
=A. Perspective, supporting reason, example, response to opposing view, final takeaway#Correct. This plan keeps the presentation focused.
~B. Topic, unrelated story, different topic, closing#Incorrect. This sequence lacks coherent focus.
~C. Funny opening, random fact, thank you#Incorrect. This does not develop a clear perspective.
~D. Source title, unrelated image, second unrelated image, claim#Incorrect. This order is confusing and unfocused.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.08 U06 L03 Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Perspective: Digital calendars help students manage deadlines. Opposing view: Some students prefer paper planners. Which response keeps focus? \{
~A. Paper planners are useless and nobody should use them.#Incorrect. This is unfair and too absolute.
~B. Calendars have dates and months.#Incorrect. This does not respond to the opposing view.
~C. Students should never choose what works for them.#Incorrect. This is unreasonable and weakens the presentation.
=D. Some students prefer paper planners, but digital calendars can still help by sending reminders and making due dates easy to update.#Correct. This acknowledges the opposing view and returns to the main point.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L03 Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which revision improves academic vocabulary in a spoken perspective? \{
~A. "Stuff gets better" instead of "revision improves clarity"#Incorrect. This makes the wording less precise.
=B. "Revision improves clarity" instead of "stuff gets better"#Correct. This uses academic vocabulary that fits the presentation.
~C. "Things are okay" instead of "evidence supports the claim"#Incorrect. This loses academic meaning.
~D. "Whatever helps" instead of "effective strategy"#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.07
Which sentence would most likely weaken coherent focus? \{
~A. This detail supports my perspective because it shows how students use feedback.#Incorrect. This sentence helps coherence.
~B. Another reason is that feedback can point out unclear evidence.#Incorrect. This supports a feedback-focused presentation.
=C. Also, school lunches should include more fruit choices.#Correct. This shifts to an unrelated topic and weakens focus.
~D. A final example is a student revising a confusing claim after peer review.#Incorrect. This stays connected to the topic.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.07 U06 L03 Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.08
A speaker uses a question at the beginning: "What would happen if every student had a clear study plan?" What is the likely purpose? \{
=A. To engage listeners and introduce the presentation's focus#Correct. An opening question can draw attention and point toward the topic.
~B. To avoid stating any main idea later#Incorrect. A question should lead into the main idea, not replace it.
~C. To confuse the audience on purpose#Incorrect. A purposeful question should support understanding.
~D. To prove that evidence is unnecessary#Incorrect. A question does not remove the need for support.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Perspective: Students should use class feedback before final submission. Which supporting detail fits best? \{
~A. Final submission buttons can be different colors.#Incorrect. This is not connected to using feedback and does not support the perspective about improving writing.
~B. Classrooms have different seating charts.#Incorrect. This detail does not support the feedback perspective.
=C. Feedback can identify unclear explanations before the assignment is graded.#Correct. This directly supports the perspective.
~D. Some students have backpacks.#Incorrect. This detail is unrelated to feedback and does not help prove why feedback improves writing.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.08 U06 L03 Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which opposing view is written fairly? \{
=A. Some students may prefer independent study because it allows them to focus without conversation.#Correct. This presents the opposing view respectfully and clearly.
~B. People who disagree do not understand anything.#Incorrect. This is unfair and dismissive.
~C. The other side is ridiculous.#Incorrect. This attacks the opposing view instead of stating it fairly.
~D. Nobody could ever have a different opinion.#Incorrect. This denies the possibility of an opposing view.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.07
A speaker has four details. Which one best supports the perspective "Academic vocabulary helps listeners understand precise ideas"? \{
~A. Some presentations happen in the morning.#Incorrect. This does not support vocabulary precision.
~B. Students sometimes use slides.#Incorrect. This does not focus on academic vocabulary.
~C. A classroom clock can be hard to see.#Incorrect. This detail is unrelated to academic vocabulary and would distract from the presentation's focus.
=D. Words such as "credible" and "coherent" name specific communication qualities.#Correct. This directly supports the perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L03 Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.08
Which word best completes this academic sentence? "A focused presentation should maintain a _____ perspective." \{
~A. random#Incorrect. Random ideas would weaken focus.
=B. coherent#Correct. "Coherent" means logically connected and fits the sentence.
~C. hidden#Incorrect. A perspective should be clear, not hidden.
~D. careless#Incorrect. Careless does not fit an academic presentation goal.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Which presentation focus is too broad? \{
~A. Peer review helps writers notice unclear explanations before final submission.#Incorrect. This is focused because it gives a specific point about peer review.
=B. School is important for many reasons.#Correct. This is too broad to guide a coherent oral presentation.
~C. Weekly planning helps students divide long assignments into smaller tasks.#Incorrect. This is focused and clear.
~D. Study groups help students compare quiz review strategies.#Incorrect. This is specific enough to guide support.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.07 U06 L03 Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
A speaker chooses examples about classmates' real study challenges. What rhetorical purpose does this choice serve? \{
~A. It removes the need for organization.#Incorrect. Relevant examples still need organization.
~B. It changes the presentation into a poem.#Incorrect. The examples do not change the communication mode.
~C. It avoids the speaker's purpose.#Incorrect. The examples likely support the speaker's purpose.
=D. It makes the presentation more relevant to the audience.#Correct. Audience-relevant examples can help the speaker achieve purpose.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.07
Which sentence best returns to the main focus after an opposing view? \{
=A. While some students prefer working alone, a brief study group can still help students test their understanding before a quiz.#Correct. This acknowledges the view and returns to the main perspective.
~B. That other view is wrong, so I will ignore it.#Incorrect. This is dismissive and does not strengthen focus.
~C. Anyway, lunch schedules are confusing.#Incorrect. This shifts to an unrelated topic.
~D. Opposing views are not allowed in presentations.#Incorrect. Opposing views can be addressed when relevant.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.08 U06 L03 Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.08
Which response weakens validity when addressing an opposing argument? \{
~A. Fairly stating the opposing view before responding#Incorrect. This usually strengthens validity.
~B. Explaining why the main perspective still makes sense#Incorrect. This supports a valid response.
=C. Misrepresenting the opposing view so it sounds weaker#Correct. Misrepresenting an opposing view weakens fairness and validity.
~D. Returning to the main claim after the response#Incorrect. This helps keep coherent focus.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Which detail supports the perspective "Oral rehearsal improves presentation clarity"? \{
~A. Some rooms have chairs.#Incorrect. This classroom detail does not connect to oral rehearsal or explain how rehearsal improves clarity.
~B. Presentation files can be saved on a computer.#Incorrect. This is not about oral rehearsal.
~C. Students sometimes use pencils.#Incorrect. This detail is unrelated to oral rehearsal and would not support the perspective about presentation clarity.
=D. Rehearsal helps speakers notice where wording sounds confusing.#Correct. This directly supports the perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L03 Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Which phrase is most precise for a presentation about opposing arguments? \{
=A. opposing perspective#Correct. This phrase precisely names the different point of view.
~B. the other stuff#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~C. whatever they say#Incorrect. This is informal and dismissive.
~D. some random idea#Incorrect. This is vague and unfair.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.07
Which presentation plan stays focused on one perspective? \{
~A. Perspective: feedback improves writing. Details: lunch menus, sports scores, notebook colors.#Incorrect. These details do not support the perspective.
=B. Perspective: feedback improves writing. Details: feedback identifies unclear claims, feedback suggests stronger evidence, feedback helps revision.#Correct. Every detail supports the same perspective.
~C. Perspective: feedback improves writing. Details: school buses, cafeteria tables, hallway signs.#Incorrect. These details are unrelated.
~D. Perspective: feedback improves writing. Details: random facts, jokes, unrelated memories.#Incorrect. This plan lacks coherent support.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.07 U06 L03 Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.R.08
A speaker chooses a respectful tone when addressing disagreement. How does that support purpose? \{
~A. It makes evidence unnecessary.#Incorrect. Tone does not replace evidence.
~B. It removes the speaker's perspective.#Incorrect. A respectful tone can still support a clear perspective.
=C. It helps the audience consider the response instead of reacting to an insult.#Correct. Respectful tone supports persuasion and clarity.
~D. It prevents organization.#Incorrect. Tone and organization can work together.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.V.01
Which sentence is a coherent final takeaway? \{
~A. That is all, and I also like music.#Incorrect. This adds an unrelated idea.
=B. Study groups are useful because they help students explain ideas, ask questions, and prepare with classmates.#Correct. This final takeaway restates the focused perspective.
~C. So, study groups, posters, and lunch all matter equally.#Incorrect. This mixes unrelated topics.
~D. The end is here now.#Incorrect. This closes the presentation but does not reinforce the perspective.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
A student asks, "Does this detail help my audience understand my perspective?" What skill is the student using? \{
=A. Checking whether support connects to the main idea#Correct. The student is testing whether details support the presentation's perspective.
~B. Avoiding coherent focus#Incorrect. This question actually helps maintain focus.
~C. Removing the audience from the presentation#Incorrect. The question includes audience awareness.
~D. Choosing random details#Incorrect. The student is doing the opposite by checking relevance.
}
